# How images are made: the imaging foundations, stages 0 to 4.
# Each sequence follows its module's own lesson. The numbers are the
# lessons' invented teaching examples, not settings for any real scan.

@ img.0.shadows
from: the module's own lesson
goal: Notice that blocking light makes a shadow, and that a shadow does not show everything about the thing that made it.
intro: A lamp shines on a wall. Hold a card in the way. Now less light reaches the wall, and a dark shape appears. That dark shape is a shadow.
  show: emoji 💡 🃏 🌑
demo: Here are two round cards, the same size. One is red and one is blue. Hold each one in front of the lamp. Their shadows look just the same! The shadow shows the shape, but not the colour.
  show: table Card | Its shadow ;; a red circle | a dark circle ;; a blue circle | a dark circle
P: What makes a shadow?
  + Something gets in the way of the light :: The card blocks the light.
  - The wall makes it by itself :: [wall-makes-it] Take the card away and the shadow goes.
  - Switching the lamp off :: [no-light] With no light, the whole wall is dark, and there is no shadow shape.
  hint: What happens when you take the card away?
  why: A shadow is where light has been blocked.
G: Could you pick it up, or is it a shadow?
  bin You could pick it up: the card | the toy | the cup
  bin It is a shadow: the dark shape of the card | the dark shape of the toy | the dark shape of the cup
  hint: Can you hold a shadow in your hand?
  why: The thing is real and you can hold it. Its shadow is a picture made by blocked light.
O: Make a shadow. Put the steps in order.
  items: Switch on the lamp | Hold the card in front of the lamp | Look at the wall | See the dark shape
  ends: First | Last
  hint: You need the light on first.
  why: Light first, then something in the way, then the shadow appears on the wall.
F: What colour is the shadow?
  frame: A red card makes a __ shadow
  taps: red | dark | blue
  answer: dark
  x red :: [shadow-has-colour] The shadow is where the light is blocked, so it is dark, not red.
  x blue :: [shadow-has-colour] The shadow does not take a colour from the card.
  hint: Look at the table of shadows.
  why: A shadow is dark, whatever the colour of the card.
>P: Two shadows on the wall look the same. Can you tell which card is red?
  + No: the shadow does not show the colour :: You need to look at the cards themselves.
  - Yes: the red card's shadow is red :: [shadow-has-colour] Both shadows are dark.
  - Yes: the bigger shadow is red :: [guessed] The cards are the same size, so the shadows are too.
  hint: What did the red card and the blue card make?
  why: A picture made by blocked light shows the outline. It can leave other things out.
done: A shadow shows a shape, not everything. Make shadows with your hands and see what they leave out.

@ img.0.inside-outside
from: the module's own lesson
goal: Tell what you can see on the outside from what is hidden inside, and change your guess when you find out more.
intro: A box is shut. You can see the outside: its colour and its shape. You cannot see what is inside. You can only guess, until you find out.
  show: emoji 📦 ❓
demo: A toy box has a picture of a ball on the lid. Sam guesses, "There is a ball inside." A grown-up opens it. Inside is a scarf! The picture was a clue, but it did not prove what was inside. Sam changes his guess.
  show: flow look at the lid > guess: a ball > open it > it is a scarf > change the guess
G: Can you see it, or is it hidden?
  bin You can see it: the colour of the box | the picture on the lid | the shape of the box
  bin It is hidden: a toy inside the shut box | a sock inside the shut drawer | a letter inside the sealed envelope
  hint: Is it on the outside, or shut away inside?
  why: We can describe the outside. What is inside stays hidden until we look.
P: The lid has a picture of a ball. What do you know for sure?
  + The lid has a picture of a ball on it :: That is what you can see.
  - There is a ball inside :: [guess-as-fact] That is only a guess until you look.
  - The box is empty :: [guess-as-fact] You cannot see inside, so you do not know that either.
  hint: Say only what you can see.
  why: What we see is one thing. What we guess is another.
O: Put the steps in order.
  items: Look at the outside | Make a guess | Open it with a grown-up | Look inside | Change your guess if you need to
  ends: First | Last
  hint: You guess before you open it.
  why: Look, guess, open, look again, and change your mind if the box surprises you.
>P: You guessed that a teddy was in the box. The grown-up opens it, and there is a book. What do you say?
  + "It is a book. My guess was wrong." :: You changed your guess when you saw.
  - "It is still a teddy." :: [ignored-the-evidence] You can see that it is a book.
  - "The box is wrong." :: [blamed-the-evidence] The box was right. The guess needed changing.
  hint: What can you see now?
  why: Good finders change their guess when they get new clues.
done: Outside, inside, guess, then look. Play a guessing game with a box at home.

@ img.1.viewpoints
from: the module's own lesson
goal: Compare views of one thing from the front, the side and the top, and choose another view to find what one view hides.
intro: A thing can look different when you look from different places. A cup seen from the top looks like a circle. From the side it looks tall. No single view shows everything.
  show: shapes circle rectangle
demo: Put two blocks in a line, one behind the other. From the front you see only one block, because the near one hides the far one. Walk round to the side, and now you can see two. The blocks did not move. Your view did.
  show: table Where you look from | Blocks you can see ;; the front | 1 ;; the side | 2
M: Match each view to what it looks like.
  pairs: A cup from the top => A circle | A cup from the side => A tall shape with a handle | A pyramid from the side => A triangle | A book from its edge => A thin rectangle
  hint: Which one has a handle?
  why: The same thing can make different pictures, depending on where you look from. A cup is a circle from the top and a tall shape from the side.
P: Two blocks stand one behind the other. You look from the front. How many blocks can you see?
  + One :: The near block hides the far one.
  - Two :: [counted-the-hidden] You know there are two, but from the front the second is hidden.
  - None :: [miscounted] You can see the near block.
  keep
  hint: Look at the table.
  why: One thing can hide another when they line up in front of your eyes.
F: Look from above.
  frame: From the top, a round cake looks like a __
  taps: circle | triangle | square
  answer: circle
  x square :: [wrong-shape] A round cake has no corners.
  x triangle :: [wrong-shape] A round cake has no corners.
  hint: Is the cake round or pointy?
  why: Seen from above, a round cake is a circle.
>P: A big box might be hiding a ball behind it. How can you find out?
  + Look from the side :: A new view can show what the first view hid.
  - Look harder from the front :: [same-view] The box is still in the way from the front.
  - Close your eyes :: [no-view] Then you cannot see anything at all.
  hint: What did we do to see the second block?
  why: When one view hides something, another view can show it.
done: Every view hides something. Draw a toy from the front, the side and the top.

@ img.1.slices
from: the module's own lesson
goal: Understand that a slice shows the inside at one place, that slices must be kept in order, and that a slice is different from the outside.
intro: Cut through a loaf of bread and look at the slice. It shows the inside of the loaf, just where you cut. A slice is a view of the inside at one place.
  show: emoji 🍞 🍎 🥚
demo: Stack three card circles: small, big, small. From the side the stack is thin at the ends and wide in the middle. Now put the big circle on top. The same pieces make a different shape. That is why slices must stay in the right order.
  show: table Order of the circles | Shape of the stack ;; small, big, small | thin, wide, thin ;; big, small, small | wide at the top
P: What does a slice of bread show?
  + The inside of the loaf, where it was cut :: A view through the middle.
  - Only the crust on the outside :: [outside-only] A slice shows the inside too.
  - The whole loaf at once :: [part-as-whole] It shows only one place in the loaf.
  hint: What can you see on a slice that you cannot see on a whole loaf?
  why: A slice shows the inside at the place where the cut went.
G: Does it show the inside, or the outside?
  bin The inside: a cut orange showing its segments | a slice of cake showing its layers | a cut egg showing the yolk
  bin The outside: the peel of an orange | the icing on top of a cake | the shell of an egg
  hint: Was it cut open?
  why: Cutting through something shows its inside. The outside is what you see without cutting.
O: An apple was cut into three slices. Put them back in order, from top to bottom.
  items: The slice with the stalk | The slice with the seeds | The slice with the bottom of the apple
  ends: Top | Bottom
  hint: Where is the stalk on an apple?
  why: Putting slices back in order rebuilds the whole shape. Mix them up, and the shape is wrong.
P: The stack was small, big, small. You move the big circle to the top. Is it still the same shape?
  + No: the order changed the shape :: Same pieces, different shape.
  - Yes: it has the same pieces :: [same-pieces-same-shape] The order of the pieces matters too.
  hint: Look at the second row of the table.
  why: The same slices in a different order make a different shape.
>P: Some scanners in hospitals make pictures that look like slices of the body. Does the scanner cut the person?
  + No: it takes measurements, and a computer builds the slice picture :: Nothing is cut.
  - Yes, very thinly :: [literal-cut] Nobody is cut. The slice is a picture made from measurements.
  - Yes, like bread :: [literal-cut] The scanner measures. It does not cut.
  hint: Is the slice picture a real cut, or a picture?
  why: A scan slice is a picture of the inside made with measurements and a computer, not a knife.
done: A slice shows the inside at one place, and the order matters. Cut an orange with a grown-up and look at the slice.

@ img.2.pixels
from: the module's own lesson
goal: Convert a distance in pixels into a real distance using the calibration, and tell enlarging an image on screen from recording more detail.
intro: A digital image is a grid of numbers. Each cell of a flat image is a pixel; each small block of a three-dimensional image is a voxel. If the image is calibrated, you know how much real distance lies between neighbouring pixel centres.
  show: grid 4x6 on=8,11
demo: In a toy image, neighbouring pixel centres are 2 mm apart. Two marked points are 6 pixel steps apart, so they are 6 × 2 = 12 mm apart in the object. Enlarging the image three times on screen makes the picture bigger. It does not make the object 36 mm across.
  show: steps 6 steps × 2 mm a step = 12 mm ;; enlarged on screen: still 12 mm
F: Convert pixels into millimetres.
  frame: With 0.5 mm between pixel centres, a distance of 8 pixel steps is __ mm
  answer: 4
  x 16 :: [divided-instead] Multiply the steps by the spacing: 8 × 0.5.
  x 8 :: [ignored-the-calibration] Eight is the number of steps. Each step is 0.5 mm.
  hint: Steps × millimetres per step.
  why: 8 × 0.5 = 4 mm.
X: Neighbouring pixel centres in this toy image are 1.5 mm apart. Count more pixel steps and watch the real distance.
  param n: 0..20 /1 @0 | Pixel steps
  out mm: 1.5*n | Real distance | mm
  aim: Find how many pixel steps span 18 mm.
  target: mm == 18
  view: bars mm over n
  hint: Divide 18 by 1.5.
  why: 12 steps × 1.5 mm = 18 mm. The calibration turns pixel steps into a real length, along the direction measured.
M: Match each term to its meaning.
  pairs: Pixel => One cell of a flat image | Voxel => A small block of a three-dimensional image | Pixel spacing => The distance between neighbouring pixel centres | Zoom => Showing the stored cells larger on the screen
  hint: "Vox" is like "volume".
  why: These words keep apart what was recorded and how it is shown.
P: You zoom in four times on a blurry image. Will you see detail that the image did not record?
  + No: zooming enlarges the stored cells and adds no new information :: The detail was never recorded.
  - Yes: zooming reveals hidden detail :: [zoom-as-resolution] Enlarging cannot create information that is not there.
  - Yes, if you zoom far enough :: [zoom-as-resolution] More zoom only makes the same cells bigger.
  hint: Where would the new detail come from?
  why: Display enlargement changes size on the screen, not what was measured.
>P: Two tiny features in an object are 1 mm apart. The image has 0.5 mm between pixel centres, but the object moved while it was being imaged. Will the image show two separate features?
  + Not necessarily: movement, blur and noise can merge them, whatever the pixel spacing :: Small spacing does not guarantee separation.
  - Yes: two pixel steps is plenty :: [spacing-as-resolution] Pixel spacing is not the same as the power to separate features.
  - No, never: images can never separate features 1 mm apart :: [overgeneralised] A sharp, still image with this spacing might.
  hint: What else, besides pixel spacing, decides whether features look separate?
  why: Resolving power depends on blur, motion and noise as well as on the grid.
done: Steps times spacing gives distance, and zoom gives size, not detail. Measure something in a photo of a ruler.

@ img.2.echoes
from: the module's own lesson
goal: Work out the distance to a boundary from the time an echo takes to return, and say what limits the estimate.
intro: Sound travels through a material. At a boundary, some of the sound bounces back as an echo. The time the echo takes tells you how far away the boundary is, if you know how fast sound travels in that material.
  show: flow a pulse goes out > it reaches a boundary > an echo comes back > time the round trip
demo: In an ideal water tank, sound travels at 1,500 m/s. A pulse returns after 0.004 s. In that time the sound travels 1,500 × 0.004 = 6 m. But it went there and back, so the boundary is 3 m away. This is an invented exercise, not a way to set up or read a medical scan.
  show: steps total path = 1,500 × 0.004 = 6 m ;; depth = 6 ÷ 2 = 3 m
P: Why do we halve the total distance?
  + The sound travelled to the boundary and back again :: The depth is half the round trip.
  - Because sound slows down by half :: [invented-reason] The speed is assumed constant. The halving is about the route.
  - We should not halve it :: [forgot-the-return] Then the depth would be twice too large.
  hint: Trace the route of the sound.
  why: depth = speed × time ÷ 2, because the time covers the outward and the return journeys.
F: Use depth = speed × time ÷ 2.
  frame: With sound at 1,500 m/s and an echo after 0.002 s, the boundary is __ m away
  answer: 1.5
  x 3 :: [forgot-to-halve] Three metres is the round trip. Halve it.
  x 750 :: [forgot-the-time] Multiply the speed by the time, then halve.
  hint: 1,500 × 0.002, then halve.
  why: 1,500 × 0.002 = 3 m there and back, so the boundary is 1.5 m away.
X: In the ideal tank, change the time the echo takes and watch the depth of the boundary.
  param t: 0..10 /0.5 @0 | Echo time | ms
  out d: 0.75*t | Depth of the boundary | m
  aim: Find the echo time for a boundary 6 m away.
  target: d == 6
  view: bars d over t
  hint: Each millisecond of round trip covers 1.5 m, which is 0.75 m of depth.
  why: An 8 ms round trip at 1,500 m/s covers 12 m, so the boundary is 6 m away. Depth grows in proportion to the echo time.
S: Select everything that can make the depth estimate wrong.
  + The real speed of sound differs from the assumed speed :: The calculation depends on it.
  + The echo is too weak to detect clearly :: Then the timing is uncertain.
  + The sound reaches the detector by a longer, bouncing route :: Then the time does not match a straight path.
  - The tank is painted blue :: [irrelevant-factor] Colour does not change the sound's journey.
  - The room is quiet :: [irrelevant-factor] Quiet helps. It does not cause an error.
  hint: What does the calculation assume?
  why: The simple formula assumes a known speed, a clear echo and one straight path. Break any of these and the estimate suffers.
>P: Sound actually travels faster in a material than the speed you assumed. Is your calculated depth too large or too small?
  + Too small :: The real sound covered more distance in the measured time.
  - Too large :: [reversed] A faster real speed means the true boundary is farther than calculated.
  - Exactly right :: [assumption-ignored] The speed assumption is part of the answer.
  hint: depth = speed × time ÷ 2. What if the real speed is larger?
  why: Using a speed that is too low gives a depth that is too small. Every echo estimate inherits its speed assumption.
done: Time the round trip, then halve it, and check the speed. Time an echo in a stairwell and estimate the distance.

@ img.3.attenuation
from: the module's own lesson
goal: Use an ideal attenuation model to calculate transmission, and explain why one projection cannot show where along a path the material lies.
intro: A transmission measurement compares the signal entering an object with the signal reaching a detector. In an ideal uniform absorber, I = I₀ × e^(−μx): I₀ is the signal going in, x the path length and μ the attenuation coefficient. The model assumes one beam energy and ignores scatter.
  show: flow incident signal I₀ > through material of length x > transmitted signal I
demo: Start with 80 units. Three ideal layers each pass half of what reaches them: 40, then 20, then 10. Swap identical layers and nothing changes. Even two different layers, passing 1/2 and 1/4, give 1/8 in either order. One measurement cannot tell which layer came first.
  show: bars Incident=80 After_one_layer=40 After_two=20 After_three=10 unit=signal_units
F: Each ideal layer passes half the signal reaching it.
  frame: 64 units through three such layers leaves __ units
  answer: 8 | eight
  x 32 :: [one-layer-only] Thirty-two is after one layer. Keep halving.
  x 16 :: [two-layers-only] Sixteen is after two layers. There are three.
  hint: 64, 32, 16, …
  why: 64 × ½ × ½ × ½ = 8.
X: Send 100 units through a uniform material with μ = 0.2 per cm. Increase the thickness and watch the transmitted signal.
  param x: 0..10 /0.5 @0 | Thickness | cm
  out i: 100*exp(-0.2*x) | Transmitted signal | units
  aim: Find the thickness that leaves about 37 units, one "attenuation length".
  target: abs(i-36.79) < 0.1
  view: plot i over x
  hint: The exponent must reach −1.
  why: At 5 cm, 0.2 × 5 = 1, and e^(−1) ≈ 0.37. Each further 5 cm cuts the signal by the same factor again.
M: Match each symbol to its meaning.
  pairs: I₀ => The signal entering the material | I => The signal reaching the detector | μ => The attenuation coefficient | x => The path length through the material
  hint: The subscript zero marks the starting value.
  why: Keeping incident and transmitted signal apart is the heart of a transmission measurement.
C: Two stacks of ideal layers give one projection each.
  left: text A layer passing 1/2 in front of a layer passing 1/4.
  right: text A layer passing 1/4 in front of a layer passing 1/2.
  + Both transmit 1/8, so this one measurement cannot tell them apart :: The projection records a total along the path.
  - The left transmits more, because the stronger layer comes first :: [order-matters-in-a-projection] Multiplication gives 1/8 in either order.
  - They cannot be compared :: [gave-up] Multiply the fractions and compare.
  hint: Multiply the two fractions in each order.
  why: A single projection sums the effect of everything along its path. Order and position are lost.
>P: A single projection shows that something along one path absorbs strongly. Can you tell how deep in the object it lies?
  + No: one projection records only the total along the path; views from other directions are needed :: That is why CT combines many projections.
  - Yes: the darker the spot, the deeper it is :: [darkness-as-depth] Depth and absorption are mixed together along the path.
  - Yes: it must be in the middle :: [guessed-position] Nothing in one projection fixes the position along the path.
  hint: Recall the two stacks that gave the same measurement.
  why: Locating material needs measurements along different paths. That is the idea behind computed tomography.
done: Transmission multiplies along a path, and one path cannot say where. Compare a shadow from the front with one from the side.

@ img.3.signals
from: the module's own lesson
goal: Name the signal that each imaging method measures, and explain why brightness in one kind of image need not mean the same thing as brightness in another.
intro: An image is a spatial display made from measurements. Different methods measure different physical signals, so their brightness values mean different things. X-rays are ionising radiation. Ultrasound is sound. MRI uses magnetic fields and radiofrequency signals, which are non-ionising.
  show: table Method | What it measures ;; X-ray projection | transmission along paths ;; CT | many projections, combined into cross-sections ;; ultrasound | returning sound echoes ;; MRI | radiofrequency signals in a magnetic field
demo: Imagine two greyscale images of an invented sample. In one, bright means strong transmission. In the other, bright means a strong echo. The same brightness on two screens does not mean the same physical measurement. Even within one method, changing the display settings changes the appearance while the recorded data stay the same.
  show: pair text Image 1: bright = strong transmission || text Image 2: bright = strong echo
M: Match each method to the signal it measures.
  pairs: X-ray projection => Transmission along each path | CT => Many projections combined into a cross-section | Ultrasound => Sound echoes returning from boundaries | MRI => Radiofrequency signals from nuclei in a magnetic field
  hint: Ultrasound is the one that listens.
  why: Each method turns a different physical signal into an image.
G: Ionising or non-ionising?
  bin Ionising: X-ray radiography | CT
  bin Non-ionising: ultrasound | MRI
  hint: Which methods use X-rays?
  why: X-ray methods use ionising radiation. Ultrasound uses sound, and MRI uses magnetic fields and radiofrequency signals.
P: The display settings of an image are changed so that it looks brighter. What has happened to the recorded data?
  + Nothing: only the mapping from numbers to shades has changed :: The measurements are the same.
  - The measurements have increased :: [display-as-data] The screen changed, not the data.
  - Some data have been deleted :: [display-as-data] Changing the display deletes nothing.
  hint: Is the brightness on the screen the same thing as the measured number?
  why: Appearance depends on display choices. The physical measurements do not.
>P: A region looks bright in an ultrasound image, and another region looks equally bright in a CT image. What can you conclude?
  + Only that each is bright for its own method: one returns a strong echo, the other attenuates X-rays strongly :: Brightness is not a shared unit.
  - That both regions are made of the same material :: [brightness-as-material] The two images measure different things.
  - That both images measured the same quantity :: [brightness-as-material] Equal brightness across methods means nothing on its own.
  hint: What does bright mean in each kind of image?
  why: An image is not a photograph. To read it, you must know what was measured.
done: Ask what was measured before you ask what it shows. Compare how two different images of the same thing were made.

@ img.4.inverse-problems
from: the module's own lesson
goal: Write a small imaging problem as y = Ax + noise, decide whether the measurements fix the unknowns, and explain how regularisation stabilises an estimate at the price of assumptions.
intro: A forward model predicts the measurements from the object: y = Ax + ε, where x holds the unknown values, A describes the measurement and ε is the error. Reconstruction runs the reasoning backwards, estimating x from y. That reverse problem can have many answers, or answers that swing wildly with small errors.
  show: flow object x > measurement process A > measurements y, with error ε > estimate of x
demo: Take two unknown cells, a and b. The single sum a + b = 10 allows (4, 6), (7, 3) and endless other splits. Add the measurement 2a + b = 14. Subtracting the first gives a = 4, and so b = 6. But if the second measurement had been 2a + 2b = 20, it would only repeat the first.
  show: steps a + b = 10 ;; 2a + b = 14 ;; subtract: a = 4 ;; so b = 6
F: Solve the two measurements.
  frame: If a + b = 9 and a + 2b = 13, then a = __
  answer: 5 | five
  x 4 :: [solved-for-b] Four is b. Then a = 9 − 4.
  x 13 :: [took-a-measurement] Thirteen is a measurement, not an unknown.
  hint: Subtract the first equation from the second to find b.
  why: Subtracting gives b = 4, and so a = 9 − 4 = 5.
P: The measurements are a + b = 10 and 2a + 2b = 20. What can be said about a and b?
  + They are not fixed: the second measurement repeats the first, so many pairs fit :: Two equations, one piece of information.
  - a = 5 and b = 5 :: [assumed-symmetry] That is one of infinitely many pairs that fit.
  - There is no solution :: [inconsistent-assumed] Many pairs fit. None is singled out.
  hint: Divide the second equation by 2.
  why: Two recorded equations are not necessarily two independent pieces of information.
X: Two measurements: a + b = 10, and a + 1.01b = 10 + e, where e is a small error in the second. The true values are a = 10 and b = 0. Add a little error and watch the estimate of b.
  param e: -0.1..0.1 /0.01 @0 | Error in the second measurement
  out b: 100*e | Estimated b
  out a: 10 - 100*e | Estimated a
  aim: Find the error that shifts the estimate of b all the way to 5.
  target: b == 5
  view: bars b over e
  hint: The two equations are nearly the same, so their difference is tiny.
  why: An error of 0.05, half of one per cent of the measurement, moves b from 0 to 5. This system is ill-conditioned: tiny errors make large changes.
M: Match each term to its meaning.
  pairs: Forward model => Predicts the measurements from the object | Inverse problem => Estimates the object from the measurements | Ill-conditioned => Small errors in the data cause large changes in the estimate | Regularisation => A penalty that stabilises the estimate by adding an assumption | Non-unique => Many objects fit the same measurements
  hint: Regularisation is a deliberate preference.
  why: These are the working ideas of image reconstruction.
>P: A reconstruction adds a penalty that favours smooth images. The noise fades. What is the risk for a small, sharp feature?
  + It may be blurred or suppressed, because the penalty treats sharpness as noise :: Stability is bought with an assumption.
  - None: regularisation only removes noise :: [penalty-without-cost] The penalty cannot tell a sharp real feature from noise.
  - The feature will become sharper :: [reversed] A smoothness penalty works against sharp edges.
  hint: What does the penalty prefer?
  why: Regularisation stabilises an estimate by building in an assumption. Where the assumption is wrong, the image is wrong.
done: Count independent measurements, and ask what the penalty assumes. Solve a 2-by-2 system and then perturb it.

@ img.4.validation
from: the module's own lesson
goal: Design a reproducible, non-clinical test of an imaging method using known objects, held-out conditions and explicit measures of error.
intro: A phantom is a test object with known features. Measuring it shows how a system behaves. Three properties must be kept apart. Bias is a systematic offset. Repeatability is how close repeated measurements are. Resolution is the ability to separate nearby features.
  show: table Property | Question ;; bias | is the average off by a fixed amount? ;; repeatability | do repeated measurements agree? ;; resolution | can nearby features be told apart?
demo: A known feature is 10 mm wide. Method A reports 11.9, 12.0 and 12.1 mm: very repeatable, but on average 2 mm too large. Method B reports 9, 10 and 11 mm: no average error in these three trials, but a wider spread. Which is more useful depends on the task, and three trials prove nothing in general.
  show: table Method | Readings | Average | Bias ;; A | 11.9, 12.0, 12.1 | 12.0 | +2 ;; B | 9, 10, 11 | 10.0 | 0
F: A method measures a 10 mm feature three times: 10.4, 10.5 and 10.6 mm.
  frame: Its average bias is __ mm
  answer: 0.5 | +0.5
  x 10.5 :: [average-as-bias] Ten and a half is the average reading. The bias is the average less the true size.
  x 0.2 :: [spread-as-bias] Two tenths is the spread of the readings, not their offset.
  hint: The average reading, minus the true 10 mm.
  why: (10.4 + 10.5 + 10.6) ÷ 3 = 10.5, and 10.5 − 10 = 0.5 mm of bias.
G: Is the problem bias, repeatability or resolution?
  bin Bias: every reading of a 10 mm feature is close to 12 mm | the method always reads 5% too large
  bin Repeatability: three scans of the same feature give 8, 10 and 13 mm | readings change from one session to the next
  bin Resolution: two dots 1 mm apart appear as a single blob | a thin gap between two features disappears
  hint: Is it a constant offset, a scatter or a merging?
  why: Each problem has its own cause and its own measure. Reporting only one hides the others.
O: Put the stages of a fair evaluation in order.
  items: State the task and the measure of error | Choose a phantom with known features | Fix the acquisition and processing settings | Test under conditions not used for tuning | Report the errors and the limits
  ends: First | Last
  hint: The measure of error is chosen before any results are seen.
  why: Defining the task and the error measure first, and testing on held-out conditions, keeps the evaluation honest.
P: A reconstruction method was tuned on one phantom, and is then tested on the same phantom. Why are the results likely to flatter it?
  + It has been fitted to that object, so performance there overstates performance on new conditions :: Testing needs held-out cases.
  - They will not: the phantom is known exactly :: [tuning-ignored] Knowing the phantom is why the method could be fitted to it.
  - They will be worse than on new objects :: [reversed] Fitting to an object makes results on it look better, not worse.
  hint: Could the method have failed this test?
  why: A test must use conditions that the method was not tuned on.
>P: A new method measures every phantom feature accurately. Its developers announce that it is "ready for diagnosing patients". What is the criticism?
  + Phantom accuracy is necessary but far from sufficient: clinical performance needs its own evidence, and this exercise claims no clinical competence :: Know the limits of the test.
  - None: accurate is accurate :: [phantom-as-clinic] Patients are not phantoms. Their anatomy, motion and disease vary.
  - Phantom tests are useless :: [overcorrection] They answer a precise question about the system. They are simply not the whole answer.
  hint: What does a phantom test leave out?
  why: Each test supports a bounded claim. Reporting the bounds is part of the result.
done: Bias, spread and separation, measured on held-out conditions. Check one measuring tool at home against something of known size.
